# Cookie Init — TODO

Cookie Init is an open-source Linux init system designed to help small Linux distribution developers build functional operating systems.

## 1. Core Initialization
- [x] Start as PID 1.
- [x] Set up the initial environment variables.
- [x] Set the system hostname.
- [x] Mount essential virtual filesystems.
- [x] Initialize the journal.
- [ ] Check that required filesystem mount points exist.
- [ ] Report initialization failures clearly.
- [ ] Handle partially failed initialization safely.

## 2. Autoexec System
- [x] Read `/etc/cookie/autoexec.list`.
- [x] Execute programs sequentially.
- [x] Wait for each program to exit before launching the next.
- [ ] Validate the autoexec list and report malformed entries.
- [ ] Handle missing or non-executable programs.
- [ ] Log each program's exit status.
- [ ] Document the autoexec file format.

## 3. Process Supervision
- [x] Restart the autoexec sequence when the supervisor returns.
- [ ] Reap orphaned child processes.
- [ ] Prevent rapid restart loops.
- [ ] Add configurable restart delays.
- [ ] Distinguish normal exits from crashes and execution failures.
- [ ] Define how persistent services should be supervised.

## 4. Journal and Logging
- [x] Write logs to `/var/log/cookie-init.log`.
- [x] Print log messages to standard error.
- [x] Support log levels.
- [x] Add the `--clearjournal` command-line option.
- [ ] Handle missing log directories.
- [ ] Include reliable timestamps.
- [ ] Handle journal write failures.
- [ ] Add configurable journal paths.
- [ ] Document journal APIs for developers.

## 5. Signals and Shutdown
- [ ] Implement safe SIGCHLD handling and child reaping.
- [ ] Handle shutdown and reboot requests.
- [ ] Shut down supervised processes in a defined order.
- [ ] Unmount filesystems safely where appropriate.
- [ ] Call the Linux reboot API for actual reboot and power-off operations.
- [ ] Ensure PID 1 remains functional when a child process fails.

## 6. Configuration and Portability
- [ ] Make the hostname configurable.
- [ ] Make startup paths configurable at build time.
- [ ] Support different root filesystem layouts.
- [ ] Document required kernel features.
- [ ] Support static builds for minimal root filesystems.
- [ ] Keep optional features independent where practical.

## 7. Testing and Reliability
- [ ] Test booting in QEMU with a minimal root filesystem.
- [ ] Test missing and invalid autoexec files.
- [ ] Test failed program execution.
- [ ] Test child crashes and orphaned processes.
- [ ] Test repeated supervisor restarts.
- [ ] Test journal failures and unavailable storage.
- [ ] Test shutdown and reboot behavior.
- [ ] Add automated tests for non-PID-1 functionality.

## 8. Documentation and Releases
- [ ] Document the architecture and process model.
- [ ] Write installation instructions.
- [ ] Document command-line options.
- [ ] Document configuration files.
- [ ] Add contribution guidelines.
- [ ] Choose and document component licensing.
- [ ] Define a versioning and release policy.

## Long-Term Ideas
- [ ] Optional service supervision.
- [ ] Optional dependency-aware startup.
- [ ] Configurable service restart policies.
- [ ] Boot-time diagnostics.
- [ ] Distribution-specific integration hooks.

---

**Priority:** PID 1 reliability, child reaping, safe supervision, and shutdown handling should come before advanced service-management features.